<!--
    Region-based (Phoenix/SAILR) structurer - last-resort edge-virtualization
    ORDERING infrastructure (option `regionedgeorder`, default OFF; SAILR P2).
    Sits on top of `regionstructure` (DIV-12 default-on): the read side of angr
    `phoenix._last_resort_refinement` (the dominance-tiered crossing/secondary/other
    bucketing via forward immediate-dominators + `to_acyclic_graph` + post-order
    `node_seq`) and `sailr._order_virtualizable_edges` (H1 sibling-count -> H2
    post-dominator count [capped at postdom_max_edges=10 / postdom_max_graph_size=50]
    -> H3 return-edge -> base node_seq tiebreak).

    What this proves end to end:

      * When the structurer MUST virtualize an edge to a goto (no acyclic/cyclic
        schema applies and >1 component remains), `regionedgeorder` ON replaces the
        flat H1/H3 + address ordering with the dominance-tiered bucketing + H2
        post-dominator heuristic.  `cross` is a reducible function with sibling
        cross-edges that the acyclic schemas decline, so the structurer reaches the
        `virtualize_one_edge` fallback - confirmed live via `KUNA_RS_DEBUG=1`
        (`[rs] VIRT ...`): the new ordering FIRES on this function and selects a
        DIFFERENT edge to virtualize than the flat ordering would.

    Source (x86-64 GCC -O1, the `.text` bytes of a `-c` object loaded at 0x401000):

        int cross(int a, int b, int c, int d, int e) {
            int s = 0;
            if (a > b) { if (c > d) goto P; s = 1; }
            else       { s = 2; P: s += e; }
            if (a) s += 10;
            return s;
        }

    PARITY PROOF (the honest standalone result): the edge-virtualization ordering
    only changes WHICH edge becomes a goto when the structurer is already FORCED to
    virtualize.  Standalone (without the P1 cyclic-natural-loop fold that lets more
    virtualized graphs converge), the kuna structurer that must virtualize on a knot
    still reports `ok=false` for the FINAL collapse and falls back to
    `CollapseStructure` to emit the C - so the net effect on emitted C is ZERO: pass
    1 (`regionedgeorder off`) and pass 2 (`regionedgeorder on`) emit byte-identical
    C.  Turning the opt-in ON exercises the new dominance-bucketing + H2
    post-dominator machinery (it FIRES and picks a different edge) without changing
    output, which is exactly the parity invariant this increment lands: the ordering
    infrastructure is wired in and active, byte-safe by construction, and the goto
    REDUCTION compounds once P1 lets virtualized graphs converge to a structured root
    instead of falling back.

    Across the whole 83-file / 675-assertion datatest corpus the structurer never
    virtualizes (every function is reducible and folds via schemas), so this ordering
    is unobservable there too - `make test` stays PARITY OK 675/675.

    Asserted invariants below hold across BOTH passes (counts are 2x the per-pass
    count): the if/else arms, the shared `+ e` join, the post-test, and the return -
    identical with the ordering off and on.
-->
